KZ2 patch 2166: a drawable prefetcher keeps the FIFO walker off the compositor.

Written for the Pyrrhus native Mac bring-up; no game code or RPCS3 code used.
Licence: MIT (project LICENSE).

CAMetalLayer.nextDrawable blocks until the compositor returns one, and a fully
occluded window gets none. The walker called it inline in the present, so while
the game window sat covered -- the scripted runs open on a working desktop -- the
walker held the FIFO drain for exactly as long as the window stayed that way:
drains of 26-56 s, the GPU idle, [pump] gap == the drain span, and a close
request could not run (one run ignored SIGTERM and was killed). The same
scripted session headless, no compositor at all, never drained longer than
961 ms (work/macos-runs/20261118-231521 against the windowed runs of the same
evening). The stalls the BUILDING note attributed to depth readbacks on the
mission load were measured windowed and are occlusion: the largest readback
any ftrace window measured was 12.8 ms.

One host thread now stays one drawable ahead. The walker's present becomes a
try-take: an empty mailbox skips the frame -- the compositor is then owed one
it was not showing anyway -- and presents resume on uncover. The blocking
nextDrawable call lives on the prefetcher thread alone. Headless and
host-surface paths are untouched, and PS3RECOMP_METAL_DRAWABLE_AHEAD=0
restores the inline call of today.

